7.6.5.2
A delta hedged strategy
Even though the simple dispersion strategy initially has very low delta exposure, as the prices of the underlying stocks
change, the deltas of the straddle positions will also change, leading to higher exposure to delta risk. One way forward
is to delta hedge the individual stock options with the underlying stock. Following the simple strategy, the long leg of
the dispersion trade has a delta exposure to stock i given by
∆L,i,t = ∆C,i,t + ∆P,i,t
where ∆C,i,t and ∆P,i,t are the BS-delta for a call and a put option on the ith stock, respectively. The short leg has a
delta exposure to stock i given by
∆S,i,t = νi,t
 ∆C,I,t + ∆P,I,t

where ∆C,I,t and ∆P,I,t are the BS-delta for a call and a put option on the index, respectively. The deltas are computed
at the market close each day using closing stock prices and index level. The delta exposure of the dispersion strategy
(DS) to stock i satisﬁes
∆DS,i,t = ∆L,i,t −∆S,i,t
which is rebalanced everyday such that the trade keeps delta-neutral until the expiration date. The return of the deltahedged disperion trading over the life of the options is given by
