for any x1, ..., xk. Thus, the property of mutual independence of random variables of a set X is some special property
of its joint distributions. The above equation is a special case of the more general property of probability distributions
P(X1 ∈B1, X2 ∈B2, ..., Xk ∈Bk) = P(X1 ∈B1)P(X2 ∈B2)...P(Xk ∈Bk)
which holds for any Borel sets B1, ..., Bk. The mathematical notion of independence in probability theory is of
