A transformation T of a probability space is mixing if for every two measurable sets A and B we have P(A ∩
T −nB) →P(A)P(B) as n →∞, and a stationary process is called mixing if the corresponding shift transformation
T is mixing. Thus, a mixing stationary process is ergodic and we can construct ergodic but non-mixing stationary
processes.
