B.3
Approximating the implied volatility surface in the MixVol model
B.3.1
An approximation to infer the implied volatility
B.3.1.1
The implied volatility
For simplicity of exposition, we let CBS(t, x, K, T; σ) be the Black-Scholes call price with volatility σ, and we assume
that the market price of an option for strike K and maturity T can be decomposed into a Black-Scoles price around
ATM forward plus some extra term α(K, T). That is,
C(t, x, K, T) = CBS(t, x, K, T; Σ(T, K)) + α(K, T)
(B.3.13)
where the volatility Σ(T, K) is a function of strike and maturity. The function α(K, T) is a modiﬁcation of the BlackScholes formula capable of generating a skew, or a curvature, or both. It must be a convex function with respect to the
strike K. For notation purpose, we let the modiﬁed price be expressed as
y = y(0) + α
where y(0) = CBS(t, x, K, T; σ) is the Black-Scholes price around-the-money forward. Given the deﬁnition of the
implied volatility in Section (1.4.1.2), we let y = C(t, x, K, T) be the call price for strike K and maturity T, and we
let g(y) = f −1(y) be the inverse function.
